WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:07.874
In this video you will learn to find your way
around Data Hall 4, using the hall map from

2
00:00:07.874 --> 00:00:08.624
the guide.

3
00:00:09.974 --> 00:00:16.849
We will look at the map, learn to read a rack
location, see the pattern inside every row,

4
00:00:16.849 --> 00:00:21.793
find the pods and special racks, and finish in
the network rows.

5
00:00:25.943 --> 00:00:29.036
This is Data Hall 4 seen from above.

6
00:00:29.786 --> 00:00:32.858
Take a moment to look at the whole map first.

7
00:00:35.058 --> 00:00:40.199
On the left, at the west end of the hall, are
network rows one and two.

8
00:00:40.949 --> 00:00:43.467
This is where many of your trunks end.

9
00:00:44.817 --> 00:00:51.586
Everything else is compute rows, numbered
three to twenty-six, running left to right

10
00:00:51.586 --> 00:00:52.795
across the map.

11
00:00:54.145 --> 00:01:01.271
The compute rows are split into three pods:
pod ten, pod eleven and pod twelve.

12
00:01:02.621 --> 00:01:06.205
Each small striped box is one TPU rack.

13
00:01:06.955 --> 00:01:12.416
The six colored stripes stand for the six
cable groups you install on it.

14
00:01:13.766 --> 00:01:17.030
The white boxes are the end of row racks.

15
00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:22.068
The orange boxes in the middle aisle are the
SPOCS racks.

16
00:01:23.418 --> 00:01:27.621
Now let us learn how each of these racks gets
its name.

17
00:01:31.771 --> 00:01:34.373
Every rack has a location code.

18
00:01:35.123 --> 00:01:39.283
You read it left to right: site, hall, row,
spot.

19
00:01:40.783 --> 00:01:42.959
The first part is the site.

20
00:01:44.309 --> 00:01:47.211
DH four means data hall four.

21
00:01:48.561 --> 00:01:51.334
R zero five means row five.

22
00:01:52.684 --> 00:01:54.561
And twelve is the spot.

23
00:01:55.311 --> 00:01:58.127
A spot is a rack's place in its row.

24
00:02:00.427 --> 00:02:04.779
So this code means hall four, row five, spot
twelve.

25
00:02:05.529 --> 00:02:08.623
That is the first SPOCS rack of pod ten.

26
00:02:09.973 --> 00:02:13.663
On the map, a rack shows only its row and
spot.

27
00:02:14.413 --> 00:02:17.272
So this rack is labeled 512.

28
00:02:18.022 --> 00:02:23.910
The last two digits are always the spot, and
everything before them is the row.

29
00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:31.623
Every compute row follows the same pattern.

30
00:02:32.373 --> 00:02:35.125
Once you know it, you can find anything.

31
00:02:36.625 --> 00:02:43.110
Spots two to ten hold nine TPU racks on the
north side of the middle aisle.

32
00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:50.348
Spot eleven is the end of row rack that serves
those nine north racks.

33
00:02:51.698 --> 00:02:54.173
Spot twelve is the middle aisle.

34
00:02:54.923 --> 00:02:58.208
In a few rows, a SPOCS rack sits here.

35
00:02:59.558 --> 00:03:07.504
Spot thirteen is the end of row rack for the
south side, and spots fourteen to twenty-two

36
00:03:07.504 --> 00:03:10.182
hold the nine south TPU racks.

37
00:03:11.532 --> 00:03:17.079
The blocks marked CDU at the row ends are not
explained in the guide.

38
00:03:17.829 --> 00:03:24.015
In liquid cooled halls a CDU usually supplies
coolant to the racks.

39
00:03:24.765 --> 00:03:26.515
You do not cable it.

40
00:03:27.865 --> 00:03:33.433
So every row has eighteen TPU racks and two
end of row racks.

41
00:03:37.583 --> 00:03:39.417
Let us use the pattern.

42
00:03:40.167 --> 00:03:45.053
Which end of row rack serves TPU rack 1517?

43
00:03:46.853 --> 00:03:48.751
First, split the number.

44
00:03:49.501 --> 00:03:53.917
1517 is row fifteen, spot seventeen.

45
00:03:55.267 --> 00:04:01.567
Spot seventeen is between fourteen and
twenty-two, so it is on the south side of the

46
00:04:01.567 --> 00:04:02.542
middle aisle.

47
00:04:03.892 --> 00:04:08.137
South racks use the end of row rack at spot
thirteen.

48
00:04:08.887 --> 00:04:11.831
So the answer is rack 1513.

49
00:04:13.181 --> 00:04:14.269
One more.

50
00:04:15.019 --> 00:04:18.817
Rack 905 is row nine, spot five.

51
00:04:19.567 --> 00:04:24.559
Spot five is north, so its end of row rack is
911.

52
00:04:28.709 --> 00:04:36.090
A pod is a group of one hundred forty-four TPU
racks that share three SPOCS racks.

53
00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:39.144
Each pod covers eight rows.

54
00:04:41.444 --> 00:04:47.759
Pod ten's three SPOCS racks are 512, 612 and
712.

55
00:04:48.509 --> 00:04:52.797
Red is number one, orange is two, and yellow
is three.

56
00:04:54.147 --> 00:04:59.736
Pod eleven uses 1312, 1412 and 1512.

57
00:05:00.486 --> 00:05:06.587
Pod twelve uses 2112, 2212 and 2312.

58
00:05:07.937 --> 00:05:14.721
Six of the end of row racks are a special
type, marked EOR BMS on the map.

59
00:05:15.471 --> 00:05:24.431
They are 611, 613, 1411, 1413, 2211 and 2213.

60
00:05:25.181 --> 00:05:29.277
You will meet them again in lessons thirteen
and fourteen.

61
00:05:33.427 --> 00:05:36.777
Finally, the network rows at the west end.

62
00:05:37.527 --> 00:05:39.511
These are rows one and two.

63
00:05:41.111 --> 00:05:45.121
The pink racks are the DCN patch panel racks.

64
00:05:45.871 --> 00:05:50.799
They come in pairs, an N rack and an S rack,
labeled A through H.

65
00:05:52.149 --> 00:05:59.936
For example, rack 107 is patch panel A north,
and 108 is patch panel A south.

66
00:06:01.286 --> 00:06:04.465
The purple racks are the DCN spines.

67
00:06:05.815 --> 00:06:12.001
And the two white racks, 131 and 204, are the
management spines.

68
00:06:13.351 --> 00:06:21.265
The racks marked oxide, SASL and FU are not
explained in the guide, and you do not install

69
00:06:21.265 --> 00:06:22.759
anything in them.

70
00:06:26.909 --> 00:06:31.411
You can now read any rack location and find it
on the map.

71
00:06:32.161 --> 00:06:39.042
In lesson two of the course, practice by
clicking racks on the interactive map and

72
00:06:39.042 --> 00:06:41.057
decoding location codes.

